skill: Java Basics

@ Core Java Fundamentals | B
Which tool compiles Java source code (.java files) into bytecode (.class files)?
= javac
- java
- javadoc
- jar
? javac is the Java compiler. The java command launches the JVM to run compiled bytecode.

@ Core Java Fundamentals | B
Why is Java described as platform independent?
= Compiled bytecode can run on any machine that has a compatible JVM
- The source code is recompiled by hand for every operating system
- The JVM is written in Java so it needs no operating system
- Java programs run without any runtime environment
? The compiler produces bytecode, and each platform provides its own JVM that executes that same bytecode.

@ Core Java Fundamentals | I
Which statement correctly describes the difference between the JDK and the JRE?
= The JDK contains development tools such as the compiler in addition to the runtime
- The JRE contains the compiler but the JDK does not
- The JDK can only run on Windows while the JRE runs anywhere
- They are identical packages with different names
? The JRE is enough to run Java programs. The JDK adds tools like javac and javadoc for developing them.

@ Data Types and Operators | B
What is the result of 7 / 2 in Java when both operands are of type int?
= 3
- 3.5
- 4
- 3.0
? Dividing two ints performs integer division and discards the fractional part, so 7 / 2 is 3.

@ Data Types and Operators | I
What is the value of x after these statements run?
  int x = 5;
  x += 3 * 2;
= 11
- 16
- 13
- 10
? Multiplication happens first: 3 * 2 = 6, then x = 5 + 6 = 11.

@ Data Types and Operators | I
What does -7 % 3 evaluate to in Java?
= -1
- 1
- 2
- -2
? In Java the result of % takes the sign of the dividend. -7 = (-2 * 3) + (-1).

@ Data Types and Operators | I
What does this statement print?
  System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3);
= false
- true
- 0.3
- A compile-time error
? 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be represented exactly in binary floating point, so the sum is 0.30000000000000004, not 0.3.

@ Data Types and Operators | A
What does this code print?
  int i = 5;
  int j = i++ + ++i;
  System.out.println(i + " " + j);
= 7 12
- 6 11
- 7 11
- 6 12
? i++ yields 5 and then i becomes 6. ++i makes i 7 and yields 7. So j = 5 + 7 = 12 and i = 7.

@ Strings | B
Which statement about the String class in Java is true?
= String objects are immutable
- A String can be modified in place with setCharAt
- String is a primitive data type
- String is mutable just like StringBuilder
? Once created, a String cannot change. Methods such as toUpperCase return a new String.

@ Strings | I
What does this code print?
  String a = "hello";
  String b = "hello";
  String c = new String("hello");
  System.out.println((a == b) + " " + (a == c));
= true false
- true true
- false false
- false true
? Identical literals share one object in the string pool, so a == b is true. new String creates a separate object, so a == c is false.

@ Strings | I
Which method compares two strings for equal content while ignoring letter case?
= equalsIgnoreCase
- compareTo
- The == operator
- isEqual
? equalsIgnoreCase compares characters ignoring case. == compares object references, not content.

@ Strings | I
Which class is the best choice for building a long string by appending inside a loop?
= StringBuilder
- Repeated String concatenation with the + operator
- Scanner
- Character
? Concatenating Strings in a loop creates many temporary objects. StringBuilder appends to one mutable buffer.

@ Arrays | B
What is the index of the last element of an array whose length is n?
= n - 1
- n
- n + 1
- 0
? Array indexes start at 0, so the last valid index is length - 1.

@ Arrays | I
What happens at runtime when you read arr[arr.length] for a non-empty array?
= An ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is thrown
- The compiler reports an error
- The value 0 is returned
- The value null is returned
? The compiler cannot know the index is invalid, but at runtime the access is out of bounds and the JVM throws the exception.

@ Control Flow | B
Which loop is guaranteed to execute its body at least once?
= do-while
- for
- while
- enhanced for
? A do-while loop checks its condition after executing the body.

@ Basic Coding | I
What does this code print?
  int sum = 0;
  for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) continue;
    sum += i;
  }
  System.out.println(sum);
= 9
- 15
- 6
- 12
? Even numbers are skipped, so sum = 1 + 3 + 5 = 9.

@ Basic Coding | I
What does this code print?
  String s = "java";
  String r = "";
  for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    r += s.charAt(i);
  }
  System.out.println(r);
= avaj
- java
- ajav
- vaja
? The loop walks the string from the last character to the first, which reverses it.

@ Basic Coding | A
What does this code print?
  int n = 2;
  switch (n) {
    case 1: System.out.print("one ");
    case 2: System.out.print("two ");
    case 3: System.out.print("three ");
    default: System.out.print("other");
  }
= two three other
- two
- two three
- other
? Without break statements execution falls through from the matching case into every following case, including default.
